## 6. CROWD AND CONGESTION
- Pedestrian crowds of ~20–50 on the right footpath near the lit shops throughout the later-night period (part 9); ~20–30 at minute 235; 6–10 loitering near white barricades at minute 249; groups of 3–8 near the right hoarding/bus shelter during minutes 250–274.
- Traffic queues formed only at red signals: minutes 667, 669, 671–672 (motorcycles, an auto-rickshaw, a dark sedan and white mini-truck waiting and moving off on green); auto-rickshaws queued at the red light at minutes 700, 701, 703, 718, 719. No jams, blockages or collisions (other than the minute 599 event) were recorded; traffic was free-flowing in all other minutes.

## 7. TRAFFIC VIOLATIONS
- Red-light jumping: minute 538 (white car, dark car and several autos from ~5s onward); minute 230 (general red-light disregard, "no signal discipline evident"); signals repeatedly showed amber/red to the camera approach with traffic flowing freely through minutes 575–649.
- Helmetless riders: repeatedly observed/apparent (minutes 210, 220, 224, 316, 575–599, 633; many others "could not be confirmed in the dark").
- Pedestrian on carriageway among moving traffic: minute 215.
- Passengers carried in open goods-truck beds: minutes 650 and 672.
- No wrong-way driving was observed. (Reversing manoeuvres at minutes 104, 108, 112, 115, 154 are listed under Notable Events.)

## 8. PATTERN OF ACTIVITY
The footage covers a full overnight cycle at one busy signalised junction. From 21:23 to midnight the junction carried steady, busy night traffic with a recurring white hatchback (left-to-right, ~30s each minute) as a near-constant feature through minutes 2–148, alongside buses, auto-rickshaws and constant two-wheelers. From roughly midnight to 05:00 (parts 11–19) traffic thinned to light, free-flowing overnight flows dominated by motorcycles and yellow-green auto-rickshaws, with goods vehicles, coaches and occasional emergency vehicles passing through; pedestrians were rare and no queues formed. From ~05:00 onward (parts 20–29) traffic built steadily with dawn: auto-rickshaws became the most common vehicle (4–15 per minute), motorcycle volumes rose to 8–20 per minute, and bus movements increased markedly (red city buses, DTC-style blue-and-white buses, double-deckers and private coaches). Goods and municipal traffic (mixers, tippers, refuse trucks, tankers, container trucks) appeared throughout but concentrated in the early-morning hours. Wet/damp road surface recurred across the whole period, indicating intermittent rain. The signals facing the camera showed amber/red for much of the period, with compliance variable overnight and queues forming normally only in the final hours. No pattern of suspicious activity, no repeat offenders and no systematic violations beyond those listed above were evident.

## 9. QUIET PERIODS
- Minutes 275–499 — consistently light, free-flowing small-hours and pre-dawn traffic with no incidents, no queues and virtually no pedestrians (parts 12–19); the quietest stretch of the recording.
- Minutes 500–574 — light free-flowing traffic, sparse pedestrians, nothing unusual (parts 21–23).
- Minutes 185, 197 and 198 — no footage available at all (frames could not be processed); these should be re-submitted for review.
